# J.P. Morgan Chase & Co. Quantitative Research Virtual Experience Program

## Overview

This repository contains my solutions for the J.P. Morgan Chase & Co. Quantitative Research Virtual Experience Program on Forage.

The project focused on applying quantitative research techniques to real-world financial problems across commodity markets and credit risk. The work involved data analysis, time series modelling, machine learning, optimisation techniques, and financial risk modelling using Python.

The repository contains implementations for:

- Natural gas price analysis and forecasting
- Commodity storage contract valuation
- Credit risk modelling and expected loss estimation
- FICO score quantisation and risk rating optimisation

---

# Project Structure

## 1. Data_Analysis_of_Natural_Gases_data.py

### Purpose

This script analyses historical natural gas price data and creates a model capable of estimating natural gas prices for both historical and future dates.

### Work Completed

- Imported and cleaned monthly natural gas price data.
- Converted date information into a usable time series format.
- Visualised historical natural gas price movements.
- Analysed seasonal patterns affecting natural gas prices.
- Built a forecasting model incorporating:
  - Historical price trends
  - Long-term movement
  - Seasonal monthly behaviour
- Generated price estimates for dates outside the original dataset range.
- Created a function that accepts a date input and returns an estimated natural gas price.

### Dataset Used

`Nat_Gas.csv`

Contains monthly natural gas prices from October 2020 to September 2024.

---

# 2. prototype pricing model.py

### Purpose

This script creates a prototype valuation model for natural gas storage contracts.

### Work Completed

The model evaluates the economic value of storing natural gas by considering:

- Injection dates
- Withdrawal dates
- Purchase prices
- Selling prices
- Injection and withdrawal rates
- Maximum storage capacity
- Storage costs
- Operational costs

The model simulates the movement of physical gas inventory through time and calculates the profitability of a storage strategy.

The function can be adjusted for different storage scenarios by changing the contract parameters.

### Example Use Case

A trader can estimate the value of buying natural gas during lower-priced periods, storing it, and selling it during periods where prices are expected to increase.

